# watch — real-world examples

Re-run a command at intervals and display the output full-screen.

## Basics

```sh
# Re-run every 2 seconds (default)
watch ls -l
watch df -h
watch lsof -u david

# Suppress the header line
watch -t ls

# Highlight what changed between updates
watch -d date

# Custom interval — every 5 seconds
watch -n 5 'lsof -u david | wc -l'
```

## Quoting matters

```sh
# Quote pipes/redirects so watch runs the whole pipeline
watch 'sudo lsof -i -P -n | grep LISTEN'
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Watch a zypper dup download in another terminal
watch -n 2 'du -sh /var/cache/zypp/packages'

# Watch Plex transcoder load on mediaserver
watch -n 1 'ps aux | grep -i [t]ranscoder'

# Wait for a host to come back after reboot — beep on success
watch -b -g 'ping -c1 -W1 homelab >/dev/null && echo UP'

# Exit automatically the moment a service state changes
watch -g 'systemctl is-active plexmediaserver'

# Keep an eye on a RAID rebuild
watch -d cat /proc/mdstat
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-n SEC` | interval between updates (default 2) |
| `-d` | highlight differences between updates |
| `-t` | no title/header |
| `-b` | beep if command exits non-zero |
| `-e` | exit if command exits non-zero |
| `-g` | exit when the output changes |
| `-c` | interpret ANSI color codes |
| `-p` | try to run at precise intervals |
| `-x` | pass command to exec instead of `sh -c` |
